An unstable-only method's type refers to a definition that is null. Reading the
unstable schema refuses the null definition itself, by name; merge.go's
buildUnstableDuplicateSet refused it later, as a missing definition, and still
guards a schema built in memory
(0049-MADR-remediate-the-2026-11-01-debugging-pass.md, G32).

-- meta.json --
{"version": 1, "agentMethods": {}, "clientMethods": {}, "protocolMethods": {"cancel_request": "$/cancel_request"}}
-- schema.json --
{"$defs": {
  "Thing": {"type": "object", "properties": {"a": {"type": "string"}}},
  "CancelRequestNotification": {"type": "object", "x-side": "protocol", "x-method": "$/cancel_request"}
}}
-- meta.unstable.json --
{"version": 1, "agentMethods": {"new": "u/new"}, "clientMethods": {}, "protocolMethods": {"cancel_request": "$/cancel_request"}}
-- schema.unstable.json --
{"$defs": {
  "NewRequest": {"type": "object", "properties": {"g": {"$ref": "#/$defs/Ghost"}}, "x-side": "agent", "x-method": "u/new"},
  "NewResponse": {"type": "object", "x-side": "agent", "x-method": "u/new"},
  "Ghost": null,
  "CancelRequestNotification": {"type": "object", "x-side": "protocol", "x-method": "$/cancel_request"}
}}
-- want.err --
parse schema.unstable.json
definition "Ghost" is null, not a schema
